WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
RETO MEIER: Google Cloud Next
2017 has hosted a lot of talks

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.690
detailing the future of cloud.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.189
If you didn't get a chance to
catch Rob Craft's introduction

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.197
to machine learning,
then stay tuned,

00:00:10.197 --> 00:00:11.280
because here is the recap.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.046
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.546 --> 00:00:17.070
COLT MCANLIS: Eric
Schmidt describes

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.890
machine learning as the next
transformation of technology.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.290
RETO MEIER: The programming
paradigm is changing.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:23.760
Instead of programming
a computer,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.470
you teach a computer
to learn something,

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.480
and it does what you want.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.760
That's important, because
90% of the world's data

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.840
is unstructured.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.729
It doesn't say,
hey, I'm a picture

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:34.650
of Reto's cat on a cat shelf.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:35.940
We just have a picture.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.860
It turns out that most
people on the internet

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.290
don't usually label things
with what they actually are.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.530
So our challenge is to
drive unstructured data

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.660
into structured understanding.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.160
Once you've got
structured data, you

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.666
can provide value on top of it.

00:00:48.666 --> 00:00:50.040
COLT MCANLIS: In
order to achieve

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.170
that, you need a few
important things,

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.230
like good quality, clean
data, which is really hard.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.320
75% of the workload for machine
learning data scientists

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.639
is doing data modeling
to get that clean data.

00:01:01.639 --> 00:01:03.180
The remaining effort
is in algorithms

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.129
to apply to that data.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:06.750
The machine learning
process takes

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.210
in data that comes in and
applies an algorithm against it

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.980
to generate predictions
that connect

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.000
the rest of your
business processes.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.200
Now, chances are that the
algorithm isn't quite right,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.100
or the data wasn't clean enough,
or you don't have enough data.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.610
You will turn these, uh,
weird scenarios into something

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.920
that you operationalize
using specialists

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.382
focusing on data cleansing
and different algorithm types.

00:01:28.382 --> 00:01:30.090
RETO MEIER: And for
some of these things,

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.470
you don't really need to
stay on the cutting edge.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.110
You can use someone
else's black boxes

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.930
and focus your own team
on specialized systems

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.750
where you've got unique
IP to lend against it.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.570
In practice, Google offers
tools across the spectrum

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.650
to help you.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.450
Firstly, there's TensorFlow--
powerful, flexible, open source

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.200
set of frameworks that
allow you to create

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.620
your own set of systems with
exactly the same code that we

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.090
use internally.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.210
The bad news is that it still
requires you to write the ML

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.040
equivalent of machine code.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.040
Now, TensorFlow is largely
meant to advance the science,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.500
but it also in many
cases is completely

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.060
sufficient for production use.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.220
COLT MCANLIS: Next
is Cloud ML Engine.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.400
This automates the most
painful parts of TensorFlow.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.100
You can let us decide
if you need 64 GPUs

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.130
and if you need 12 of those
in the US or 14 in Asia,

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.580
how many SSD I/O ops per second
you need, and the how long

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.140
you'll likely need it.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.020
We currently support T-shirt
sizing your environment

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.705
in small, medium, and
large, which means,

00:02:27.705 --> 00:02:30.180
I want it eventually, or
it's a trivial problem;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.277
or it's a reasonable problem,
and I want it reasonably

00:02:33.277 --> 00:02:34.860
quickly and I'm
willing to pay for it;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.200
or I really want
it now, and I don't

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.030
care how hard the problem is.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.640
RETO MEIER: Finally, there
is the black box section

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.000
with pre-trained models for
translation, vision, and video.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.010
But in the near
future, we expect

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.920
to have a solution that slots
in here between the final two

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.100
options, where you
can add the capability

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.560
to identify something specific
using your own data set.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.360
And this is just the
tip of the iceberg.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.890
If you'd like more
details on that,

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.840
you should check
out the full video.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.480
And if you want more recaps
on great Next content,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.639
make sure to check out
the rest of our playlist.

00:03:05.639 --> 00:03:07.680
And don't forget, the next
world tour coming soon

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:08.760
to a city near you.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

